from: optimizer-pd-role-ceiling
session: 2 more test-plan scenarios found post-review — multi-analyzer RoleCapacities aggregation

## What changed

Dean asked whether the test suite covered "all these corner cases" after the numerator fix
(911e13b7). Answering that honestly surfaced a real gap not in the plan doc's § Test plan list:
every existing P/D test (including all 6 originally-planned scenarios) uses exactly one analyzer
entry in `s []NamedAnalyzerResult`. In production, saturation *and* throughput can both populate
`RoleCapacities` for the same disaggregated model, and they can disagree — nothing exercised that.

Added two tests in `69c759a1` (committed):

1. **`achievedSoFar`/`denom` must read only the saturation entry's `RoleCapacities`**, even when
   a second analyzer (throughput) populates a divergent `RequiredCapacity` for the same role —
   matching the existing `saturationEntry` single-canonical-source convention used elsewhere
   (`cost_aware_optimizer.go:303-311`). Verified by hand-breaking `saturationRoleView` to merge
   every analyzer's `RoleCapacities` instead of filtering to saturation, confirming the new test
   fails, then reverting.
2. **`roleBottleneckReplicas`' cross-analyzer `max` for `n_role` sizing is unaffected** by
   narrowing the achieved/denom source to saturation alone — a companion check that I didn't
   accidentally over-narrow a *different*, unrelated code path (this one intentionally still
   aggregates across every analyzer in `s`) while fixing the first issue.

## Update the plan doc

`planning/optimizer-pd-role-ceiling-plan.md` § Test plan lists 6 scenarios (all now implemented,
per the plan's own numbering) plus a "should keep passing unchanged" note. Please add a 7th/8th
item (or a new sub-list) covering the above — multi-analyzer `RoleCapacities` divergence wasn't
anticipated in the original design discussion, so it's net-new scope rather than a resolution of
an existing open question.

## Open questions / follow-ups

One deliberately-left gap, flagged but not tested (low priority, noted in my status file): a role
with zero variants (`RoleCapacities` has an entry but `variantsForRole` returns empty) — `pick()`
would return `("", 0)` immediately. Low-probability in production (every real role has ≥1
variant); leaving untested unless the planner wants it added.
